#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162744} :activity/id #uuid "2faf287c-6117-590f-9c73-9dc1ca650d9b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050266 :topic/id #uuid "3af6b95a-292c-5d96-81be-58bbf073174a" :topic/math-academy-id 3023} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163177 :tutorial/content "**The chi-square distribution** with $k$ **degrees of freedom**, denoted $\\chi^2(k)$, is the distribution of a random variable that is the sum of the squares of $k$ independent standard normal random variables.\n\nThus, if $Z_1, Z_2, \\ldots, Z_k$ are mutually independent, where $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$, then\n$Z_1^2+Z_2^2+\\ldots+Z_k^2\\sim \\chi^2(k)$.\n\nIt can be shown that $X \\sim \\chi^2(k)$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{(1)/(2^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2}))x^{k/2 - 1}e^{-x/2},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nNote that $\\Gamma$ is the gamma function.\n\nThe diagram below shows $f(x)$ for various degrees of freedom $k$.\n\n![](images/ce/cecc3fc82e618f5b5f91e61c4dcab6c1d38b970af20f0ae2baf929b0ce9cdbb3.png)\n\nThe chi-square distribution is widely used in inferential statistics. In particular, it is used in **goodness of fit** tests, where experimental data is compared against a hypothesized theoretical model. You will learn more about the applications of the chi-square distribution in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "8adfa155-2603-5b6f-9206-c73523985df0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10602 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2249706e-9b5b-5da2-bc3a-0a2a5588a700" :step/math-academy-id 217601 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162745}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162745 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161732 :question/id #uuid "531bdbe2-35a5-599f-8009-f65888b6f05d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16428"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f80bc6ce-5b86-5883-a2bb-dbdc1e5503de" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162237}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability for a Chi-Square Random Variable Using the PDF"} :step/id #uuid "352f1f5d-a28b-5167-a9a4-4b4ead2412ae" :step/math-academy-id 217602 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163178 :tutorial/content "Let $X\\sim\\chi^2(3)$. Suppose we wish to know the specific value $x$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\geq x) = 0.05\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 0.05\n$$\n\nis sometimes called the **significance level**. The required probability is represented by the shaded region below.\n\n![](images/34/34551dd57f17cfb0bdd3bebd2d09c907615f3d936e4b5d69083517d17c1f779f.png)\n\nWe often resort to percentage points tables to look up our desired values rather than computing these probabilities by hand (which would be very difficult).\n\nSome percentage points for the $\\chi^2(3)$ distribution are given below. Each cell in the table gives a value $x$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\geq x) = p\n$$\n\nfor a particular value of $p$.\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $\\,6.251\\,$ | $\\,7.815\\,$ | $\\,9.348\\,$ | $\\,11.345\\,$ | $\\,12.838\\,$ |\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 7.815) = 0.05\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x = 7.815$." :tutorial/id #uuid "fad9ff3a-2abf-529e-b457-a9b2a3922621" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10603 :tutorial/title "Working With a Percentage Points Table"} :step/id #uuid "2f6a8301-e0ac-56f8-b16c-4cfeffffbf6b" :step/math-academy-id 217605 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161733 :question/id #uuid "1c328fe8-ba46-51f0-acb3-db3781a9d859" :question/math-academy-id "e-16429"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c91d137-d318-5d98-9dc4-0b701a767b5e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162240}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find a Chi-Square Probability"} :step/id #uuid "84f7b979-7b8d-5bfd-8f98-e8e476f25db4" :step/math-academy-id 217606 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162748}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161925 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161734 :question/id #uuid "42104104-5483-5c90-be3f-dbe7b10cbff3" :question/math-academy-id "e-16430"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec19c717-37bc-53c3-b09e-21a2fd2b77fa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162241}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Chi-Square Probability Using the Complement"} :step/id #uuid "eded01af-a1e8-5d72-b095-7ea6b9995e46" :step/math-academy-id 217609 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161926 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161735 :question/id #uuid "ab5d8857-fdb8-5f4f-85cc-ba9d9e480081" :question/math-academy-id "e-16432"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a75e6c3-16e3-50ab-bd36-a2c83ba9288a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050116}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162490}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability Involving Sums of Squared Standard Normal Variables"} :step/id #uuid "d2184a6e-f98c-5e40-9508-232f506261eb" :step/math-academy-id 217613}] :activity/title "The Chi-Square Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162177}]}
